DAY 14 – NESTED LOOPS AND PATTERNS

1. NESTED LOOPS

A loop inside another loop is called a nested loop.

The outer loop controls the main iterations.
The inner loop executes for each iteration of the outer loop.


2. OUTER LOOP AND INNER LOOP

Outer loop → Controls rows.

Inner loop → Controls columns or items inside each row.

The inner loop completes its execution for every
iteration of the outer loop.


3. ROWS AND COLUMNS

In pattern programs:

Outer loop → Number of rows.

Inner loop → Number of columns or elements in each row.

Understanding rows and columns is important for solving
pattern problems.


4. SQUARE PATTERN

A square pattern contains an equal number of rows
and columns.

Each row contains the same number of elements.


5. RECTANGLE PATTERN

A rectangle pattern contains rows and columns,
but the number of rows and columns can be different.


6. INCREASING TRIANGLE

In an increasing triangle pattern, the number of
elements increases from one row to the next.

The number of elements depends on the row number.


7. DECREASING TRIANGLE

In a decreasing triangle pattern, the number of
elements decreases from one row to the next.


8. NUMBER PATTERNS

Nested loops can be used to generate different
patterns using numbers.

The value printed can depend on the row or column.


9. ALPHABET PATTERNS

Nested loops can also be used to generate patterns
using alphabets.

The alphabet printed can depend on the row or column.


10. PYRAMID PATTERN

A pyramid pattern is created by controlling both
spaces and elements according to the row number.

The number of spaces and elements changes
from row to row.


11. HOLLOW SQUARE PATTERN

A hollow square pattern prints elements only on
the boundary.

The inside portion remains empty.

Boundary conditions are used to identify the
first row, last row, first column and last column.


12. IMPORTANT PATTERN LOGIC

Outer loop → Controls rows.

Inner loop → Controls columns/elements.

Row number → Can determine the number of elements.

Column number → Can determine what is printed.

Spaces → Used to control alignment in patterns.


KEY TAKEAWAY

Nested loops are the foundation of pattern programs.

Outer loop controls rows.

Inner loop controls columns or elements.

Understanding the relationship between rows and columns
makes pattern problems easier to solve.